Here's a complete example of an MCP server with tools wrapping a REST API, using stdio transport.

---

### **Program.cs** — stdio transport with DI

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: route logs to stderr so stdout stays clean for JSON-RPC
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("api", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### **ApiTools.cs** — tool class wrapping a REST API

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a post by its numeric ID.")]
    public async Task<string> GetPost(
        [Description("The numeric post ID (1-100)")] int postId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching post {PostId}", postId);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/posts/{postId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("List all posts by a specific user.")]
    public async Task<string> GetPostsByUser(
        [Description("The user ID to filter posts by")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/posts?userId={userId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool(ReadOnly = false, Destructive = false),
     Description("Create a new post.")]
    public async Task<string> CreatePost(
        [Description("Title of the new post")] string title,
        [Description("Body content of the post")] string body,
        [Description("Author's user ID")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { title, body, userId });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
        var response = await client.PostAsync("/posts", content, cancellationToken);
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class | Services injected via primary constructor (shown above) |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services added as method params — SDK resolves them automatically |

**Key rules:**
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service are **auto-injected** — they never appear in the tool's JSON schema that the LLM sees.
- Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.
- Register services in `Program.cs` normally (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton`, etc.) — the MCP SDK resolves them from the same DI container.

**Constructor injection** (above) is preferred when tools share state or need multiple services. **Method injection** works for simpler stateless tools:

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class SimpleTools
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Ping a URL.")]
    public static async Task<string> Ping(
        [Description("URL to ping")] string url,
        HttpClient httpClient,              // ← injected by DI, hidden from LLM
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var resp = await httpClient.GetAsync(url, cancellationToken);
        return $"Status: {(int)resp.StatusCode}";
    }
}
```

To scaffold this project quickly:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
# then replace the generated files with the code above
```
